In this lesson we'll look at how often the graphs of sine, cosine, and tangent repeat themselves. A function is called periodic when it keeps coming back to the same value after a fixed distance along the x-axis, and we call that distance the period. Imagine walking along the graph of sine, you'll see the same wave shape every two pi units, so we say sine has a period of two pi. Cosine does the same thing, its period is also two pi. Tangent is a little different though, because it has those vertical asymptotes breaking the curve into separate branches. Each branch is exactly half the length of a sine wave, so tangent finishes its pattern after just pi units. That's why we say tangent has a period of pi. There are also some handy identities that come from shifting by one full period. Sine and cosine both flip to their negative when you shift by pi, but tangent just comes back to itself. Finally, we can check what happens when we plug in a negative angle. Sine gives back the negative of the original value, so it's an odd function, symmetric about the origin. Cosine gives back the same value, so it's even, symmetric about the y-axis. And tangent behaves like sine, also odd. Now let's see it in action.